Lowering Germany’s eligibility threshold by 25% drives the plan up in 2026, expanding the scope to a larger, more active cohort and higher output. The scenario shows more arrivals, a higher average active rate, more people served, and bigger value overall. {{plan.year}} remains the target year for the rebuild, and the relevant scenario metrics reflect the uplift.

The main reason is that the threshold cut expands reach and engagement, lifting arrivals_scenario, avg_active_scenario, served_scenario, and uplift_value_scenario, which together push total_value and net_value toward a higher level. The numbers in the scenario confirm a broader, more intensive program with greater revenue potential, while costs rise accordingly to support the larger plan. {{Germany.arrivals_scenario}} {{Germany.avg_active_scenario}} {{Germany.served_scenario}} {{Germany.uplift_value_scenario}} {{Germany.net_value_scenario}} {{Germany.total_cost_scenario}}.

Practically, expect the cost envelope to expand in line with the bigger plan, and keep monitoring the service_rate and notices to ensure delivery remains efficient and within thresholds. The scenario maintains a 100.0% service_rate_scenario and notes_scenario tracking, but total_cost_scenario and notices_scenario show the delta from base. {{Germany.service_rate_scenario}} {{Germany.notices_scenario}} {{Germany.total_cost_scenario}}
